description:
Change under review: let partner dashboards read account data from the browser. Added files are shown in full. For changed files, only the changed region is shown; the rest of the file is unchanged and compiles.

`src/http/cors.ts` (added):
```ts
1 import type { Handler } from "./types.js";
2
3 const PARTNER_ORIGINS = new Set(["https://acme.example.com", "https://globex.example.com"]);
4
5 export function withCors(handler: Handler): Handler {
6   return async (req) => {
7     const origin = req.headers.get("origin") ?? "";
8     const res = req.method === "OPTIONS" ? new Response(null, { status: 204 }) : await handler(req);
9     if (PARTNER_ORIGINS.has(origin)) {
10       res.headers.set("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", origin);
11       res.headers.set("Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "true");
12       res.headers.set("Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "content-type");
13       res.headers.set("Access-Control-Allow-Methods", "GET");
14     }
15     res.headers.append("Vary", "Origin");
16     return res;
17   };
18 }
```

`src/server.ts` (changed, line 22):
```ts
22 router.get("/api/account/*", withCors(accountRoutes));
```

The two partner dashboards are run by the company itself on those exact origins. The account API authenticates with the `session` cookie (`Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=None`), and the routes under `/api/account/` are read-only: `GET /api/account/profile` (name and email address) and `GET /api/account/invoices`.
